Z||000000||ITB 2M4||11-29-2001||11:37 AM||||Anyone know of somebody that can reprogram an ECM for race motors?  They have recently allowed us to work the ECM's in our class, because they didn't really know how to police them anyway.  I'm hoping it will get me a few more hp to play with.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||5
Z||000001||Nashco||11-29-2001||12:33 PM||||What kind of engine mods do you have, and what does your class allow for engine mods?  You may not need to reprogram your prom.  I'm assuming you're running a manual trans, right?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||11-29-2001||03:22 PM||||I'm afraid that you are on your own if you are running the 4 cylinder engine because I do not know of an editer for that ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ITB 2M4||11-29-2001||11:55 PM||||The rules say you can go a maximum of .040 over, and balance and blueprint the motor.  My motor is .030, with an early style 2.5 head.  Headman header, Holley timing gear, Z-28 valve springs, and port matching the head to the gasket up to 1 inch in.  Not a lot of mods allowed, just make the engine as close to perfect stock specs as possilble.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||11-30-2001||12:31 AM||||Unless you're running a bunch of advance with the holley cam, or if you want to try to give the ignition a  bunch of advance and run really rich, there's really not much a modified chip can do for ya.  In such a limited class it sounds like you'd be better off spending time trying to cut down on weight as much as you can.  Sorry I can't be much help, just doesn't sound like a custom PROM would be worth your time and money compared to other options.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
